<edge source="iran_gulf_maritime_tensi::energy_market_risk_premi" target="euro_security_realignmen::layoff_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A higher geopolitical risk premium raises energy input costs and borrowing costs for energy‑intensive European firms, squeezing margins and prompting short‑term workforce reductions to preserve cash.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">iran_gulf_maritime_tensi</data>
  <data key="d39">euro_security_realignmen</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_gulf_maritime_tensi::energy_market_risk_premi" target="global_monetary_tighteni::monetary_policy_expect" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.65</data>
  <data key="d36">An increased geopolitical risk premium in energy markets leads market participants to price a higher probability of sustained inflation or supply shocks, shifting market-implied paths toward earlier or larger central-bank policy tightening.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">iran_gulf_maritime_tensi</data>
  <data key="d39">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_gulf_maritime_tensi::energy_market_risk_premi" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::policy_change_momentum" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A sustained rise in the geopolitical risk premium for energy markets creates acute economic-security pressure that accelerates domestic regulatory agendas (investment screening, export controls, industrial policy) related to critical technologies, increasing the rate at which new tech‑governance measures are proposed and advanced.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">iran_gulf_maritime_tensi</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
