<edge source="global_monetary_tighteni::policy_rate_level" target="euro_security_realignmen::corporate_cost_pressure" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">An increase in policy rates raises short‑term borrowing and rollover costs for firms (and the cost of floating‑rate debt), directly increasing interest expense and compressing operating margins and cost pressures within months.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
  <data key="d39">euro_security_realignmen</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_monetary_tighteni::policy_rate_level" target="iran_gulf_maritime_tensi::asymmetric_attack_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A rise in policy rates (particularly US rates) tightens dollar liquidity and raises Tehran's cost of access to finance and trade, incentivizing Iran to escalate asymmetric maritime attacks and sabotage to extract leverage and revenue amid financing stress.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_maritime_tensi</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_monetary_tighteni::policy_rate_level" target="russia_ukraine_war::military_logistics_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Higher policy rates raise sovereign and commercial borrowing costs for governments, defense contractors and freight operators, constraining working capital and slowing procurement and transport of fuel, munitions and spare parts to front-line units.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
