<edge source="euro_security_realignmen::corporate_cost_pressure" target="global_monetary_tighteni::gov_bond_yield_10y" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Higher corporate cost pressure (from supply disruptions, retooling, or energy shocks) raises default and funding stress in the corporate sector, widening credit and liquidity premia and spilling over into higher sovereign 10‑year yields as investors demand greater compensation.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">euro_security_realignmen</data>
  <data key="d39">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
</edge>
<edge source="euro_security_realignmen::corporate_cost_pressure" target="global_monetary_tighteni::cross_border_flows" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.55</data>
  <data key="d36">Sustained margin compression and profitability shocks in the corporate sector reduce foreign investor appetite for regional equities and credit, triggering net portfolio outflows from the affected markets.</data>
  <data key="d37">3-12 months</data>
  <data key="d38">euro_security_realignmen</data>
  <data key="d39">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
</edge>
<edge source="euro_security_realignmen::corporate_cost_pressure" target="iran_gulf_maritime_tensi::asymmetric_attack_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising cost pressures prompt commercial shipowners and energy firms to cut spending on armed guards, convoy fees and vulnerability-reduction measures for Gulf transits, increasing the pool of softer targets and therefore the rate of asymmetric attacks on ships and facilities.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">euro_security_realignmen</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_maritime_tensi</data>
</edge>
<edge source="euro_security_realignmen::corporate_cost_pressure" target="iran_gulf_maritime_tensi::diplomatic_engagement_le" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Heightened corporate losses and insurance/route-cost shocks produce concentrated lobbying from major European shipping, energy and trade firms that forces governments to increase high‑level diplomatic outreach and de‑escalation efforts with Gulf states and Iran to protect commercial flows.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">euro_security_realignmen</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_maritime_tensi</data>
</edge>
<edge source="euro_security_realignmen::corporate_cost_pressure" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::reg_scrutiny_tech" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising corporate cost pressure forces cash‑strapped European tech firms to sell assets or accept inbound investment from non‑EU buyers (including Chinese acquirers), triggering US and allied authorities to broaden export controls and merger/review scrutiny of sensitive cross‑border technology transfers.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">euro_security_realignmen</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
